= Designing an Obsidian Vault
<designing-an-obsidian-vault>
Most Obsidian vaults grow by creating new folders whenever a note no
longer fits, so the resulting hierarchy reflects the order in which
problems appeared rather than how the notes are actually used. While
this approach is fast, renaming or moving files later, especially in a
vault whose structure is still evolving, can be error-prone. Designing
a knowledge graph and establishing the folder layout before you begin
using Obsidian is therefore the crucial first step in my personal
experience.

A reliable foundation can be obtained by using the following
cross-platform PowerShell
script #link("https://github.com/sevehub/PowerShellUtils/blob/main/Obsidian/Create-ObsidianVault.ps1")[Create-ObsidianVault.ps1].
It scaffolds a top-level hierarchy of seven purpose-driven
directories:

#image("Figures/obsidian-table.png")

Numeric prefixes enforce a stable ordering in the file explorer,
ensuring that the logical workflow remains consistent as the vault
expands.

= Blog with Parallel Editors: Obsidian-Vim
<blog-with-parallel-editors-obsidianvim>
In a
#link("https://sevehub.github.io/blog/2026W33_typst-vim9script-a-journey-to-a-new-self-published-blog.html")[previous blog post],
I described how Typst and a custom Vim9script power the engine of a
static blog website. Here, I want to discuss why using two
#strong[Parallel Editors] as a true 1:1 Markdown link between Obsidian
and Vim can be beneficial. Both applications read and write the same
plain Markdown files, the same note can therefore be opened in
Obsidian showing the rich preview/graph view, or in Vim, which provides
fast, modal editing. Any change made in one editor instantly appears in
the other.

Within Obsidian, drafts live in a richly linked graph, letting
fragmented insights connect and mature. Opening the same Markdown file
in Vim provides fast modal editing and the possibility to then promotes
the draft version into a `.typ` file, ready for the final build.

The publishing stage is fully automated via GitHub Actions. A push of
the promoted file to the repository's main branch triggers a workflow
that spins up a runner, invokes the Typst compiler, generates static
HTML, and deploys it to GitHub Pages. By offloading compilation to the
cloud, the local workstation remains a pure creative space.

= AI Work Log
<ai-work-log>
The second recurring element is the weekly tally of the
#link("https://a.co/d/0dUounbt")[AI Work Log], which is generated by a
separate application from the cards saved after each chat. The tally is
pasted into one note per ISO week. The core Daily notes plugin can
create these notes when its date format is set to `GGGG-[W]WW`, where
`GGGG` is the ISO week-year and `WW` the zero-padded ISO week number,
which yields file names such as `2026-W42.md`. The first request in a
week creates the note from a template, and later requests open the same
file. Below, you find an example of such tally.

