= Zettelkasten

The Zettelkasten folder is not free-form. It follows the numbering of
the #link("https://a.co/d/0eLGUuli")[Index Card notebook], where an
octagonal map carries eight nodes, each ticked from 1 to 20. The vault
mirrors this scheme with eight folders, `N1` to `N8`, and one note per
card side, named exactly as the printed reference.

The graph view is used to reproduce the map. One colour group per node
is defined with a path query, for example `path:30_Zettelkasten/N3/`, so
that the eight categories appear as eight colours. This is a built-in
feature and requires no plugin.

#image("Figures/Pasted image 20261015134229.jpg")

= Skills and Prompts
<skills-and-prompts>
The folder `50_Skills_Prompts` contains a single note for each skill or prompt.  
Each note is created from the `Skill‑Prompt` template (saved in the `Templates` folder by the setup script). The template defines four fields:
1. Name – the title of the skill or prompt
2. Description – a brief overview of its purpose and usage
3. Model – the LLM that should process the prompt (e.g., `gpt‑4o`, `claude‑3.5`)
4. Temperature – the sampling temperature to control output randomness

To generate a new skill note, invoke Insert template from Obsidian's
core Templates plugin (enabled by default). The template inserts the
four properties; below them you write the actual prompt text. This keeps
every skill entry consistently structured and instantly searchable
across the vault.

= Conclusion
<conclusion>
The central achievement of this architecture is pre-determining every
name in the vault as far as the design stage allows. The nine
top-level folders, the eight Zettelkasten node directories, the card
labels such as `N3-14F`, and the weekly files like `2026-W42.md` all
stem from fixed conventions: the paper notebook's numbering system and
the ISO-week calendar. The only cost is a brief setup phase: run a
one-time script, add a tiny custom plugin, and enable a few Obsidian
settings that cannot be preset. After that bootstrap, the vault's
structure remains wholly prescribed, eliminating the need for any later
ad-hoc naming or folder creation.
